<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Tucson,_Arizona> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Tucson** (; Template:Lang-es, Template:Lang-ood is a city in and the county seat of [Pima County](:Pima_County%2C_Arizona), [Arizona](:Arizona), United States, and is home to the [University of Arizona](:University_of_Arizona). It is the second largest city in [Arizona](:Arizona) behind [Phoenix](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona), with a population of 542,629 in the [2020 United States census](:2020_United_States_Census), while the population of the entire Tucson [metropolitan statistical area](:United_States_metropolitan_statistical_area) (MSA) is 1,043,433. The **Tucson MSA** forms part of the larger Tucson-Nogales [combined statistical area](:List_of_Combined_Statistical_Areas) (CSA). Both Tucson and [Phoenix](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona) anchor the [Arizona Sun Corridor](:Arizona_Sun_Corridor). The city is 108 miles (174 km) southeast of [Phoenix](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona) and 60 mi (97 km) north of the [U.S.–Mexico border](:U%2ES%2E%E2%80%93Mexico_border). Tucson is the [34th largest](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) city and the [53rd largest](:List_of_metropolitan_areas_of_the_United_States) metropolitan area in the United States (2014).Major incorporated suburbs of Tucson include [Oro Valley](:Oro_Valley%2C_Arizona) and [Marana](:Marana%2C_Arizona) northwest of the city, [Sahuarita](:Sahuarita%2C_Arizona) south of the city, and [South Tucson](:South_Tucson%2C_Arizona) in an enclave south of downtown. Communities in the vicinity of Tucson (some within or overlapping the city limits) include [Casas Adobes](:Casas_Adobes%2C_Arizona), [Catalina Foothills](:Catalina_Foothills%2C_Arizona), [Flowing Wells](:Flowing_Wells%2C_Arizona), [Midvale Park](:Midvale_Park), [Tanque Verde](:Tanque_Verde%2C_Arizona), [Tortolita](:Tortolita%2C_Arizona), and [Vail](:Vail%2C_Arizona). Towns outside the Tucson metro area include [Benson](:Benson%2C_Arizona) to the southeast, [Catalina](:Catalina%2C_Arizona) and [Oracle](:Oracle%2C_Arizona) to the north, and [Green Valley](:Green_Valley%2C_Arizona) to the south.Tucson was founded as a military fort by the Spanish when [Hugo O'Conor](:Hugo_Ocon%C3%B3r) authorized the construction of [Presidio San Agustín del Tucsón](:Presidio_San_Augustin_del_Tucson) in 1775. It was included in the state of [Sonora](:Sonora) after Mexico gained independence from the [Spanish Empire](:Spanish_Empire) in 1821. In 1853, the United States acquired a 29,670 square miles (76,840 km2) region of present-day southern [Arizona](:Arizona) and [southwestern New Mexico](:New_Mexico) from Mexico under the [Gadsden Purchase](:Gadsden_Purchase). Tucson served as the capital of the [Arizona Territory](:Arizona_Territory_%28CSA%29) from 1867 to 1877. Tucson was Arizona's largest city by population during the territorial period and early statehood, until it was surpassed by [Phoenix](:Phoenix%2C_Arizona) by 1920. Nevertheless, population growth remained strong during the late 20th century. In 2017, Tucson was the first American city to be designated a \"City of Gastronomy\" by [UNESCO](:UNESCO).The Spanish name of the city, *Tucsón* Template:IPA-es, is derived from the [O'odham](:O%CA%BCodham_language) *Cuk Ṣon* Template:IPA-azc, meaning \"(at the) base of the black [hill]\", a reference to a basalt-covered hill now known as [Sentinel Peak](:Sentinel_Peak_%28Arizona%29). Tucson is sometimes referred to as \"The Old [Pueblo](:Pueblo)\", and \"Optics Valley,\" the latter referring to its optical science and telescopes known worldwide."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
