<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Richmond,_Virginia> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Richmond** () is the capital city of the [Commonwealth of Virginia](:Virginia) in the [United States](:United_States). It is the center of the Richmond [Metropolitan Statistical Area](:Metropolitan_Statistical_Area) and the [Greater Richmond Region](:Greater_Richmond_Region). Richmond was incorporated in 1742 and has been an [independent city](:Independent_city_%28United_States%29) since 1871. At the [2010 census](:2010_United_States_Census), the city's population was 204,214; in 2020, the population had grown to 226,610, making Richmond the [fourth-most populous city in Virginia](:List_of_cities_in_Virginia). The Richmond Metropolitan Area has a population of 1,260,029, the [third-most populous metro in the state](:Virginia_statistical_areas).Richmond is at the [fall line](:Atlantic_Seaboard_fall_line) of the [James River](:James_River), 44 mi (71 km) west of [Williamsburg](:Williamsburg%2C_Virginia), 66 mi (106 km) east of [Charlottesville](:Charlottesville%2C_Virginia), 91 mi (146 km) east of [Lynchburg](:Lynchburg%2C_Virginia) and 92 mi (148 km) south of [Washington, D.C.](:Washington%2C_D%2EC%2E) Surrounded by [Henrico](:Henrico_County%2C_Virginia) and [Chesterfield](:Chesterfield_County%2C_Virginia) counties, the city is at the intersections of [Interstate 95](:Interstate_95_in_Virginia) and [Interstate 64](:Interstate_64_in_Virginia) and encircled by [Interstate 295](:Interstate_295_%28Virginia%29), [Virginia State Route 150](:Virginia_State_Route_150) and [Virginia State Route 288](:Virginia_State_Route_288). Major suburbs include [Midlothian](:Midlothian%2C_Virginia) to the southwest, Chesterfield to the south, [Varina](:Varina%2C_Virginia) to the southeast, [Sandston](:Sandston%2C_Virginia) to the east, [Glen Allen](:Glen_Allen%2C_Virginia) to the north and west, [Short Pump](:Short_Pump%2C_Virginia) to the west and [Mechanicsville](:Mechanicsville%2C_Virginia) to the northeast.The site of Richmond had been an important village of the [Powhatan Confederacy](:Powhatan_Confederacy), and was briefly settled by English colonists from [Jamestown](:Jamestown%2C_Virginia) from 1609 to 1611. The present city of Richmond was founded in 1737. It became the capital of the [Colony and Dominion of Virginia](:Colony_and_Dominion_of_Virginia) in 1780, replacing Williamsburg. During the [Revolutionary War](:American_Revolution) period, several notable events occurred in the city, including [Patrick Henry](:Patrick_Henry)'s \"[Give me liberty, or give me death!](:Give_me_liberty_or_give_me_death)\" speech in 1775 at [St. John's Church](:St%2E_John%27s_Episcopal_Church_%28Richmond%2C_Virginia%29), and the passage of the [Virginia Statute for Religious Freedom](:Virginia_Statute_for_Religious_Freedom) written by [Thomas Jefferson](:Thomas_Jefferson). During the [American Civil War](:American_Civil_War), Richmond was the capital of the [Confederacy](:Confederate_States_of_America). It entered the 20th century with one of the world's first successful electric [streetcar](:Streetcar) systems. The [Jackson Ward](:Jackson_Ward) neighborhood is a traditional hub of [African-American](:African-American) commerce and culture.Richmond's economy is primarily driven by law, finance, and government, with federal, state, and local governmental agencies, as well as notable legal and banking firms in the [downtown area](:Downtown_Richmond%2C_Virginia). The city is home to both a [U.S. Court of Appeals](:United_States_Court_of_Appeals_for_the_Fourth_Circuit), one of 13 [such courts](:United_States_courts_of_appeals), and a [Federal Reserve Bank](:Federal_Reserve_Bank_of_Richmond), one of 12 [such banks](:Federal_Reserve_System). There are several [*Fortune* 500](:Fortune_500) companies headquartered in the city including: [Dominion Energy](:Dominion_Energy), [WestRock](:WestRock), [Performance Food Group](:Performance_Food_Group), [CarMax](:CarMax), ARKO, and [Altria](:Altria) with others, such as [Markel](:Markel_Corporation), in the metropolitan area.The city continues to struggle with exceptionally high rates of murder and violent crime, making it one of the most dangerous cities in the United States."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
