<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Milwaukee_Brewers> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Milwaukee Brewers** are an American [professional baseball](:Professional_baseball) team based in [Milwaukee](:Milwaukee). They compete in [Major League Baseball](:Major_League_Baseball) (MLB) as a member club of the [National League](:National_League) (NL) [Central division](:National_League_Central). The Brewers are named for the city's association with the [brewing industry](:Brewing_industry). Since 2001, they have played their home games at [American Family Field](:American_Family_Field), which was named Miller Park through the 2020 season and has a seating capacity of 41,900 people.The team was founded in 1969 as the [Seattle Pilots](:Seattle_Pilots), an [expansion team](:Expansion_team) of the [American League](:American_League) (AL), in [Seattle](:Seattle), [Washington](:Washington_%28state%29). The Pilots played their home games at [Sick's Stadium](:Sick%27s_Stadium). After only one season, the team [relocated](:Relocation_of_professional_sports_teams) to Milwaukee, becoming known as the Brewers and playing their home games at [Milwaukee County Stadium](:Milwaukee_County_Stadium). In 1998, the Brewers joined the National League. They are the only franchise to play in four different divisions since the advent of divisional play in Major League Baseball in 1969. They are also one of two current MLB franchises to switch leagues in the modern era (1900–present), the other being the [Houston Astros](:Houston_Astros).The Brewers' only [World Series](:World_Series) appearance came in [1982](:1982_World_Series), and they are one of only six current MLB teams that have never won the World Series. After winning the [1982 American League Championship Series](:1982_American_League_Championship_Series) (ALCS) against the [California Angels](:California_Angels), the Brewers faced off against the [St. Louis Cardinals](:St%2E_Louis_Cardinals) in the World Series, losing 4–3. In 2011, the Brewers defeated the [Arizona Diamondbacks](:Arizona_Diamondbacks) to win the [2011 National League Division Series](:2011_National_League_Division_Series) (NLDS), 3–2, but lost in the [National League Championship Series](:2011_National_League_Championship_Series) (NLCS) to the eventual World Series champion Cardinals, 4–2. In 2018, the Brewers swept the [Colorado Rockies](:Colorado_Rockies) in the [NLDS](:2018_National_League_Division_Series), 3–0, but lost the [NLCS](:2018_National_League_Championship_Series) to the [Los Angeles Dodgers](:Los_Angeles_Dodgers), 4–3.From 1969 through 2022, the Brewers' overall win–loss record is 4,123–4,391 (.484)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
