<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chicago_White_Sox> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Chicago White Sox** are an American professional [baseball](:Baseball) team based in [Chicago](:Chicago). The White Sox compete in [Major League Baseball](:Major_League_Baseball) (MLB) as a member club of the [American League](:American_League) (AL) [Central](:American_League_Central) division. The team is owned by [Jerry Reinsdorf](:Jerry_Reinsdorf), and plays its home games at [Guaranteed Rate Field](:Guaranteed_Rate_Field), located on Chicago's [South Side](:South_Side%2C_Chicago). The White Sox are one of two MLB teams based in Chicago, the other being the [Chicago Cubs](:Chicago_Cubs) of the [National League](:National_League) (NL) [Central](:National_League_Central) division.One of the American League's eight charter [franchises](:Major_North_American_professional_sports_teams), the White Sox were established as a major league baseball club in [1900](:1900_in_baseball) as the **Chicago White Stockings**, before shortening their name to the White Sox in [1904](:1904_in_baseball). The team originally played their home games at [South Side Park](:South_Side_Park) before moving to [Comiskey Park](:Comiskey_Park) in [1910](:1910_in_baseball), where they played until[1990](:1990_in_baseball). They moved into their current home, which was originally also known as Comiskey Park like its predecessor and later carried sponsorship from [U.S. Cellular](:U%2ES%2E_Cellular), for the 1991 season.The White Sox won the [1906 World Series](:1906_World_Series) with a defense-oriented team dubbed \"the Hitless Wonders\", and the [1917 World Series](:1917_World_Series), led by [Eddie Cicotte](:Eddie_Cicotte), [Eddie Collins](:Eddie_Collins) and [Shoeless Joe Jackson](:Shoeless_Joe_Jackson). The [1919 World Series](:1919_World_Series) was marred by the [Black Sox Scandal](:Black_Sox_Scandal), in which several members of the White Sox were accused of conspiring with gamblers to [fix games](:Match_fixing). In response, the new MLB [Commissioner](:Commissioner_of_Baseball) [Kenesaw Mountain Landis](:Kenesaw_Mountain_Landis) banned the players from the league for life. In [1959](:1959_Chicago_White_Sox_season), the White Sox, led by [Early Wynn](:Early_Wynn), [Nellie Fox](:Nellie_Fox), [Luis Aparicio](:Luis_Aparicio), and manager [Al López](:Al_L%C3%B3pez), won the [American League pennant](:List_of_American_League_pennant_winners). They won the [AL pennant in 2005](:2005_Chicago_White_Sox_season), and went on to win the [World Series](:2005_World_Series), led by MVP [Jermaine Dye](:Jermaine_Dye), [Paul Konerko](:Paul_Konerko), [Mark Buehrle](:Mark_Buehrle), catcher [A. J. Pierzynski](:A%2E_J%2E_Pierzynski), and [Ozzie Guillén](:Ozzie_Guill%C3%A9n), the first [Latino](:Latinos) manager to win the World Series. The 87 years it took the White Sox to win the World Series stands as the second longest MLB championship drought of all time, with their crosstown rivals, the Chicago Cubs, holding the longest drought (107 seasons). The White Sox have an overall record of 9,411–9,309 (.503). Of the original sixteen teams prior to the expansion era, the White Sox have the least amount of league pennants with [five](:List_of_World_Series_champions)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
