<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Seattle_Mariners> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Seattle Mariners** are an American [professional baseball](:Professional_baseball) team based in [Seattle](:Seattle). They compete in [Major League Baseball](:Major_League_Baseball) (MLB) as a member club of the [American League](:American_League) (AL) [West division](:American_League_West). The team joined the American League as an [expansion team](:Expansion_team) in [1977](:1977_Major_League_Baseball_expansion) playing their home games in the [Kingdome](:Kingdome). Since July [1999](:1999_in_baseball), the Mariners' home [ballpark](:Baseball_park) has been [T-Mobile Park](:T-Mobile_Park), located in the [SoDo](:SoDo%2C_Seattle) neighborhood of Seattle.The \"Mariners\" name originates from the prominence of [marine](:Seamanship) culture in the city of Seattle. They are [nicknamed](:List_of_baseball_nicknames) the **M's**, a title featured in their primary logo from 1987 to 1992. They adopted their current team colors – [navy blue](:Navy_blue), northwest green ([teal](:Teal%29), and [silver](:Silver_%28color%29) – prior to the 1993 season, after having been [royal blue](:Royal_blue) and [gold](:Gold_%28color%29) since the team's inception. Their [mascot](:List_of_Major_League_Baseball_mascots) is the [Mariner Moose](:Mariner_Moose).The franchise did not field a winning team until 1991 and further success eluded them until [1995](:1995_in_baseball) when they won their first division championship and defeated the [New York Yankees](:New_York_Yankees) in the [ALDS](:American_League_Division_Series). The [game-winning hit in Game 5](:The_Double_%28Seattle_Mariners%29), in which [Edgar Martínez](:Edgar_Mart%C3%ADnez) drove home [Ken Griffey Jr.](:Ken_Griffey_Jr%2E) to win the game in the 11th inning, clinched a series win for the Mariners, served as a powerful impetus to preserve baseball in Seattle, and has since become an iconic moment in team history.The Mariners won 116 games in [2001](:2001_Seattle_Mariners_season), which set the American League record for most wins in a single season and tied the [1906 Chicago Cubs](:1906_Chicago_Cubs_season) for the Major League record for most wins in a single season. The team would not make the postseason again until [2022](:2022_Seattle_Mariners_season), which was the longest active drought in the four major North American sports.As of 2022, the franchise has finished with a losing record in [30 of 46 seasons](:List_of_Seattle_Mariners_seasons). The Mariners are the only active MLB franchise to have never appeared in the [World Series](:World_Series), and currently hold [the longest active World Series appearance drought in MLB](:List_of_Major_League_Baseball_franchise_postseason_droughts%23World_Series_appearance_droughts).As of 2022[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Seattle_Mariners&action=edit), the Mariners' all-time win–loss record is 3,402–3,783 (.473)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
