<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Guadalajara> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Guadalajara** ( [*GWAH-də-lə-HAR-ə*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key), Template:IPA-es is a [metropolis](:Metropolis) in western [Mexico](:Mexico) and the capital of the [state](:List_of_states_of_Mexico) of [Jalisco](:Jalisco). According to the 2020 census, the city has a population of 1,385,629 people, making it the 7th largest city by population in Mexico, while the [Guadalajara metropolitan area](:Guadalajara_metropolitan_area) has a population of 5,268,642 people, making it the [third-largest metropolitan area](:Metropolitan_areas_of_Mexico%23List_of_metropolitan_areas_in_Mexico_by_population) in the country and the [twentieth largest metropolitan area in the Americas](:List_of_metropolitan_areas_in_the_Americas) Guadalajara has the second-highest [population density](:Population_density) in Mexico, with over 10,361 people per square kilometer. Within Mexico, Guadalajara is a center of business, arts and culture, technology and tourism; as well as the economic center of the [Bajío](:Baj%C3%ADo) region. It usually ranks among the 100 most productive and globally competitive cities in the world. It is home to numerous landmarks, including [Guadalajara Cathedral](:Guadalajara_Cathedral), the [Teatro Degollado](:Teatro_Degollado), the [Templo Expiatorio](:Templo_Expiatorio_del_Sant%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento), the UNESCO World Heritage site [Hospicio Cabañas](:Hospicio_Caba%C3%B1as), and the [San Juan de Dios Market](:San_Juan_de_Dios_Market)—the largest [indoor market](:Marketplace) in Latin America.Guadalajara was founded on 14 February 1542 by [Cristóbal de Oñate](:Crist%C3%B3bal_de_O%C3%B1ate), a Basque [conquistador](:Conquistador), as the capital of the [Kingdom of Nueva Galicia](:Nueva_Galicia), part of the [Viceroyalty of New Spain](:Viceroyalty_of_New_Spain). After 1572, the [Royal Audiencia of Guadalajara](:Royal_Audiencia_of_Guadalajara), previously subordinate to [Mexico City](:Mexico_City), became the only authority in New Spain with autonomy over Nueva Galicia, owing to rapidly growing wealth in the kingdom following the discovery of [silver](:Silver). By the 18th century, Guadalajara had taken its place as Mexico's second largest city, following mass colonial migrations in the 1720s and 1760s. During the [Mexican War of Independence](:Mexican_War_of_Independence), independence leader [Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla](:Miguel_Hidalgo_y_Costilla) established Mexico's first revolutionary government in Guadalajara in 1810. The city flourished during the *[Porfiriato](:Porfiriato)*, with the advent of the [industrial revolution](:Industrial_revolution), but its growth was hampered significantly during the [Mexican Revolution](:Mexican_Revolution). In 1929, the [Cristero War](:Cristero_War) ended within the confines of the city, when President [Plutarco Elías Calles](:Plutarco_El%C3%ADas_Calles) proclaimed the *Grito de Guadalajara*. The city saw continuous growth throughout the rest of the 20th century, attaining a metro population of 1 million in the 1960s and surpassing 3 million in the 1990s.Guadalajara is a gamma [global city](:Global_city), and one of Mexico's most important cultural centers. It is home to numerous mainstays of [Mexican culture](:Culture_of_Mexico), including [Mariachi](:Mariachi), [Tequila](:Tequila), and [Birria](:Birria) and hosts numerous notable events, including the [Guadalajara International Film Festival](:Guadalajara_International_Film_Festival), the most important [film festival](:Film_festival) in Latin America, and the [Guadalajara International Book Fair](:Guadalajara_International_Book_Fair), the largest [book fair](:Book_fair) in the Americas. The city was the [American Capital of Culture](:American_Capital_of_Culture) in 2005 and has hosted numerous global events, including the [1970 FIFA World Cup](:1970_FIFA_World_Cup), the [1986 FIFA World Cup](:1986_FIFA_World_Cup), the 1st [Ibero-American Summit](:Ibero-American_Summit) in 1991, and the [2011 Pan American Games](:2011_Pan_American_Games). The city is home to numerous universities and research institutions, including the [University of Guadalajara](:University_of_Guadalajara) and the [Universidad Autónoma de Guadalajara](:Universidad_Aut%C3%B3noma_de_Guadalajara), two of the highest-ranked universities in Mexico."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
