<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bruce_Bullock> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Bruce John Bullock** (born May 9, 1949) is a Canadian former professional [ice hockey](:Ice_hockey) [goaltender](:Goaltender) who spent parts of three seasons in the [National Hockey League](:National_Hockey_League) in the 1970s with the [Vancouver Canucks](:Vancouver_Canucks).Playing for [Clarkson University](:Clarkson_Golden_Knights_men%27s_ice_hockey), Bullock was one of the most decorated college goaltenders of his era. He was named to the [NCAA](:NCAA) [First Team All-American](:List_of_Division_I_AHCA_All-American_Teams%23All-American_teams%23First_Team) in goal in both [1970](:1969%E2%80%9370_NCAA_Division_I_men%27s_ice_hockey_season) and [1971](:1970%E2%80%9371_NCAA_Division_I_men%27s_ice_hockey_season) (succeeding [Hall of Famer](:Hockey_Hall_of_Fame) [Ken Dryden](:Ken_Dryden), who took the honor from 1967–69), and was named [ECAC](:Eastern_College_Athletic_Conference) [Player of the Year](:List_of_ECAC_Hockey_Player_of_the_Year) in 1971. He also led Clarkson to the [1970 NCAA title game](:1970_NCAA_Division_I_Men%27s_Ice_Hockey_Tournament), where they lost to [Cornell](:Cornell_Big_Red_men%27s_ice_hockey).Following his college career, Bullock was signed by the Vancouver Canucks during training camp, and assigned to their farm team, the [Rochester Americans](:Rochester_Americans). He was then loaned to the [Chicago Black Hawks](:Chicago_Black_Hawks)' farm team in [Dallas](:Dallas_Black_Hawks). When a position became available at the Canucks' other farm team, the [Seattle Totems](:Seattle_Totems), he was assigned there where he completed his rookie pro season. He caught a break in [1972–73](:1972%E2%80%9373_NHL_season), when an injury to Canuck starter [Dunc Wilson](:Dunc_Wilson) forced his recall from the minors. He appeared in 13 games for Vancouver, posting a 3–8–3 record with a 4.79 GAA, until his season ended due to a broken finger, requiring surgery.Bullock spent another four seasons in Vancouver's organization, but never saw substantial NHL action, partially due to hand injuries. He made one start for the club in the [1974–75](:1974%E2%80%9375_NHL_season) campaign, and another appearance in [1976–77](:1976%E2%80%9377_NHL_season). During his minor league career he helped the Seattle Totems upset the [USSR national team](:Soviet_Union_national_ice_hockey_team) in an exhibition game, and was instrumental in helping the [Tulsa Oilers](:Tulsa_Oilers_%281964%E2%80%9384%29) win the [Adams Cup](:Adams_Cup_%28ice_hockey%29) in [1975–76](:1975%E2%80%9376_CHL_season). He was ultimately released by Vancouver in 1977 to make room in the system for high draft picks [Glen Hanlon](:Glen_Hanlon) and [Murray Bannerman](:Murray_Bannerman), and played two more seasons with the [Phoenix Roadrunners](:Phoenix_Roadrunners_%28PHL%29) in the [Pacific Hockey League](:Pacific_Hockey_League) before retiring in 1979.In 16 NHL appearances, Bullock posted a 3–9–3 record with a 4.79 GAA."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
