<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dubrovnik> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Dubrovnik** (Template:IPA-sh, historically known as **Ragusa** (Template:IPA-it; [see notes on naming](:Dubrovnik%23Names%29), is a city on the [Adriatic Sea](:Adriatic_Sea) in the region of [Dalmatia](:Dalmatia), in the southeastern [semi-exclave](:Semi-exclave) of [Croatia](:Croatia). It is one of the most prominent [tourist destinations](:Tourist_destination) in the [Mediterranean](:Mediterranean_Sea), a [seaport](:Port) and the centre of the [Dubrovnik-Neretva County](:Dubrovnik-Neretva_County). Its total population is 42,615 (2011 census). In 1979, the city of Dubrovnik was added to the [UNESCO](:UNESCO) list of [World Heritage Sites](:World_Heritage_Site) in recognition of its outstanding medieval architecture and fortified old town.The history of the city probably dates back to the 7th century, when the town known as *Ragusa* was founded by refugees from [Epidaurum](:Epidaurum) (*[Ragusa Vecchia](:Cavtat)*). It was under the [protection](:Protectorate) of the [Byzantine Empire](:Byzantine_Empire) and later under the sovereignty of the [Republic of Venice](:Republic_of_Venice). Between the 14th and 19th centuries, Dubrovnik ruled itself as a [free state](:Free_state_%28polity%29). The prosperity of the city was historically based on [maritime trade](:Trade); as the capital of the maritime [Republic of Ragusa](:Republic_of_Ragusa), it achieved a high level of development, particularly during the 15th and 16th centuries, as it became notable for its wealth and skilled [diplomacy](:Diplomacy). At the same time, Dubrovnik became a cradle of [Croatian literature](:Croatian_literature).The entire city was almost destroyed when a [devastating earthquake](:1667_Dubrovnik_earthquake) hit in 1667. During the [Napoleonic Wars](:Napoleonic_Wars), Dubrovnik was occupied by the [French Empire](:First_French_Empire) forces, and then the Republic of Ragusa was abolished and incorporated into the [Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy](:Kingdom_of_Italy_%28Napoleonic%29) and later into the [Illyrian Provinces](:Illyrian_Provinces). Later on, in the early 19th to early 20th century, Dubrovnik was part of the [Kingdom of Dalmatia](:Kingdom_of_Dalmatia) within the [Austrian Empire](:Austrian_Empire). Dubrovnik became part of the [Kingdom of Yugoslavia](:Kingdom_of_Yugoslavia) immediately upon [its creation](:Creation_of_Yugoslavia), and it was incorporated into its [Zeta Banovina](:Zeta_Banovina) in 1929, before becoming part of the [Banovina of Croatia](:Banovina_of_Croatia) upon [its creation in 1939](:Cvetkovi%C4%87%E2%80%93Ma%C4%8Dek_Agreement). During [World War II](:World_War_II_in_Yugoslavia), it was part of the [Axis](:Axis_powers) [puppet state](:List_of_World_War_II_puppet_states) [Independent State of Croatia](:Independent_State_of_Croatia), before being reincorporated into [SR Croatia](:Socialist_Republic_of_Croatia) in [SFR Yugoslavia](:Socialist_Federal_Republic_of_Yugoslavia).In 1991, during the [Croatian War of Independence](:Croatian_War_of_Independence), Dubrovnik was [besieged](:Siege_of_Dubrovnik) by the [Yugoslav People's Army](:Yugoslav_People%27s_Army) for seven months and suffered significant damage from shelling. After undergoing repair and restoration works in the 1990s and early 2000s, it re-emerged as one of the Mediterranean's top tourist destinations, as well as a popular filming location."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
