<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Atlanta_Braves> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Atlanta Braves** are an American [professional baseball](:Professional_baseball) team based in the [Atlanta metropolitan area](:Atlanta_metropolitan_area). The Braves compete in [Major League Baseball](:Major_League_Baseball) (MLB) as a member club of the [National League](:National_League) (NL) [East division](:National_League_East). The Braves were founded in [Boston](:Boston), [Massachusetts](:Massachusetts), in 1871, as the **Boston Red Stockings**. After various name changes, the team eventually began operating as the **Boston Braves** in 1912, which lasted for most of the first half of the 20th century. Then, in 1953, the team [moved](:Relocation_of_professional_sports_teams) to [Milwaukee, Wisconsin](:Milwaukee), and became the **Milwaukee Braves**, followed by their move to Atlanta in 1966.The name \"Braves\" originates from [a term for a Native American warrior](:Braves_%28Native_Americans%29). They are [nicknamed](:List_of_baseball_nicknames) \"the Bravos\", and often referred to as \"[America's Team](:America%27s_Team%23Other_uses)\" in reference to the team's games being broadcast nationally on [TBS](:Braves_TBS_Baseball) from the 1970s until 2007, giving the team a nationwide fan base.The Braves and the [Chicago Cubs](:Chicago_Cubs) are the National League's two remaining charter franchises. The team states it is \"the oldest continuously operating [professional sports franchise in America](:Major_professional_sports_teams_of_the_United_States_and_Canada).\"From 1991 to 2005, the Braves were one of the most successful teams in baseball, winning an unprecedented 14 consecutive division titles, making a MLB record eight consecutive [National League Championship Series](:National_League_Championship_Series) appearances, and producing one of the greatest pitching rotations in the history of baseball including Hall of Famers [Greg Maddux](:Greg_Maddux), [John Smoltz](:John_Smoltz), and [Tom Glavine](:Tom_Glavine). Since their debut in the National League in 1876, the franchise has won an MLB record 22 divisional titles, 18 [National League](:National_League_Championship_Series) [pennants](:Pennant_%28sports%29), and four World Series championships – in [1914](:1914_World_Series) as the Boston Braves, in [1957](:1957_World_Series) as the Milwaukee Braves, and in [1995](:1995_World_Series) and [2021](:2021_World_Series) as the Atlanta Braves. The Braves are the only Major League Baseball franchise to have won the World Series in three different home cities. At the end of the 2022 season, the Braves' overall win–loss record is 10,921–10,818 (.502)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
