<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Houston_Oilers> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**[National Football League](:National_Football_League) ([1970](:1970_NFL_season)–[1996](:1996_NFL_season%29)**The **Houston Oilers** were a professional American football team that played in [Houston](:Houston) from its founding in [1960](:1960_Houston_Oilers_season) to [1996](:1996_Houston_Oilers_season) before relocating to [Memphis](:Memphis%2C_Tennessee), and later [Nashville, Tennessee](:Nashville%2C_Tennessee) becoming the [Tennessee Titans](:Tennessee_Titans). The Oilers began play in 1960 as a charter member of the [American Football League](:American_Football_League) (AFL). The team won two AFL championships before joining the NFL as part of the [AFL–NFL merger](:AFL%E2%80%93NFL_merger) in the late 1960s.The Oilers competed in the AFL's [East division](:AFC_East) – along with the [Buffalo Bills](:Buffalo_Bills), the [New York Jets](:New_York_Jets) and the [Boston Patriots](:Boston_Patriots) – before the merger, after which they joined the newly formed [AFC Central](:AFC_Central). Throughout their existence the team was owned by [Bud Adams](:Bud_Adams). For the majority of their time in Houston, the team played their home games at the [Astrodome](:Astrodome), while [Jeppesen Stadium](:Jeppesen_Stadium) and [Rice Stadium](:Rice_Stadium_%28Rice_University%29) hosted the team for their first eight years.The Houston Oilers were the first champions of the American Football League, winning the [1960](:1960_American_Football_League_Championship_Game) and [1961](:1961_American_Football_League_Championship_Game) contests, but they never won another championship. The Oilers appeared in the [1962 AFL Championship](:1962_American_Football_League_Championship_Game), losing in double overtime to their in-state rivals, the [Dallas Texans](:Dallas_Texans_%28AFL%29) (now the [Kansas City Chiefs](:Kansas_City_Chiefs%29); they also won the AFL East Division title in [1967](:1967_Houston_Oilers_season) and qualified for the [AFL Playoffs in 1969](:1969_American_Football_League_playoffs), both times losing to the [Oakland Raiders](:Oakland_Raiders). From [1978](:1978_Houston_Oilers_season) to [1980](:1980_Houston_Oilers_season), the Oilers, led by [Bum Phillips](:Bum_Phillips) and in the midst of the [Luv Ya Blue](:Luv_Ya_Blue) campaign, appeared in the [1978](:1978_Houston_Oilers_season) and [1979](:1979_Houston_Oilers_season) [AFC Championship Games](:AFC_Championship_Game), but lost both. The Oilers were a consistent playoff team from [1987](:1987_Houston_Oilers_season) to [1993](:1993_Houston_Oilers_season), an era that included both of the team's only division titles ([1991](:1991_Houston_Oilers_season) and [1993](:1993_Houston_Oilers_season%29), as well as the dubious distinction of being on the losing end of [the largest comeback in NFL history](:The_Comeback_%28American_football%29). For the rest of the Oilers' time in [Houston](:Houston), however, they compiled losing seasons in almost every other year.The Houston Oilers' main colors were Columbia blue and white, with scarlet trim, while their logo was a simple [derrick](:Derrick). Oilers jerseys were always Columbia blue for home and white for away. The helmet color was Columbia blue with a white derrick from [1960](:1960_Houston_Oilers_season) through [1965](:1965_Houston_Oilers_season), silver with a Columbia blue derrick from [1966](:1966_Houston_Oilers_season) through [1971](:1971_Houston_Oilers_season), and Columbia blue with a white and scarlet derrick from [1972](:1972_Houston_Oilers_season) through [1974](:1974_Houston_Oilers_season), before changing to a white helmet with a Columbia blue derrick beginning in [1975](:1975_Houston_Oilers_season) and lasting the remainder of the team's time in [Houston](:Houston).Owner [Bud Adams](:Bud_Adams), who had openly threatened to move the team since the late 1980s, relocated the Houston Oilers to [Tennessee](:Tennessee) after the 1996 season, where they were known as the Tennessee Oilers for the [1997](:1997_Tennessee_Oilers_season) and [1998](:1998_Tennessee_Oilers_season) seasons. The Tennessee Oilers played the 1997 season in [Memphis](:Memphis%2C_Tennessee) before moving to [Nashville](:Nashville%2C_Tennessee) in 1998. In 1999, to coincide with the opening of their [new stadium](:Nissan_Stadium), Adams changed the team name to the [Tennessee Titans](:Tennessee_Titans) and the color scheme from Columbia Blue, Scarlet, and White to Titans Blue, Navy, White, and Silver with scarlet accents. The franchise retained the Houston Oilers' team history and records, while the team name was officially retired by then-[NFL Commissioner](:NFL_Commissioner) [Paul Tagliabue](:Paul_Tagliabue), thus preventing a future [Houston](:Houston), [Texas](:Texas) [NFL](:National_Football_League) team from using the Houston Oilers' name.The [NFL](:National_Football_League) returned to [Houston](:Houston), [Texas](:Texas) in [2002](:2002_Houston_Texans_season) with a new franchise, the [Houston Texans](:Houston_Texans)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
